WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:05.879
We are working with a list of state capitals
that we could use in a quiz-like game.

00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:11.730
Let’s see some list operations we can apply
to the list, specifically operations that

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:16.230
add items to the list and operations that
remove items from the list.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:20.420
As we go through each built-in method used
to affect a list, we should pay close attention

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:23.240
to the differences among these methods.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:33.020
To start, I have three state capitals added
to the 126-list_operations file for the first

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:37.910
three states in the United States alphabetically,
on line 1.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:40.910
I want to add Sacramento to the list next.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:51.140
I will, on line 2, add this statement: capitals.append(“Sacramento”)
to add the capital of California to the list.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:58.420
Then, with a print statement for the capitals
variable already in place on line 4, I will

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:00.870
run this code.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.689
The list prints as expected.

00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:12.390
Here is a general programming note: in programming,
a period (.) serves as the "accessor" that

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:16.110
allows you to reach into an object and use
one of its methods.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:19.780
Think of it like opening a toolbox to use
a specific tool.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:27.000
For example, in capitals.append(), capitals
is the object (or "toolbox") and append is

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.240
the method (or "tool").

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:34.220
The period is the mechanism that lets you
access the append method specifically from

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.170
the capitals object.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:43.250
Therefore, methods are functions that are
tied to a specific object and are invoked

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:46.180
using this period notation.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:51.219
Whereas append adds an item to the end of
a list, the insert method inserts an item

00:01:51.219 --> 00:01:54.420
at a specified position.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:02:01.310
In adding capital cities to our list, I forgot
that Arkansas and its capital, Little Rock,

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:05.350
must be inserted before Sacramento.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:11.760
Using the zero-based positioning Python has,
I want Little Rock to be the fourth capital,

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.640
so I will insert this text into position three.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:30.349
I will add a line after the append statement
and type: capitals.insert(3, “LittleRock”).

00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:34.560
I will rerun the code.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.500
So far, all these list operations are working
perfectly.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:44.310
Next, let’s see how to change the value
of a list item.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:50.410
I forgot to put a space between Little and
Rock, so I want to change that capital.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:58.120
Little Rock is at position 3 in the list,
so I will move to line 5 in the code and type

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:08.250
capitals[3] = “Little Rock” with a space,
to make that change.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:15.030
Then, using the print(capitals) statement
on line 6, I will run this code, and see the

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:16.760
updated list.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:23.180
Let’s now look at two ways we can remove
items from a list.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:27.530
First, we can remove an item by value.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:36.860
I will add a statement on line 4 with this
text: capitals.remove(“Juneau”).

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:43.620
Let’s rerun this code.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:47.010
We have a problem now, don’t we?

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:54.129
Little Rock is on the list twice, and Sacramento
is no longer there.

00:03:54.129 --> 00:03:55.129
Why is that?

00:03:55.129 --> 00:04:00.220
Take a moment to do the first part of the
Review 1.2 exercise in the workbook to determine

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:05.140
why you think this happened, and then in the
next video, we will solve this problem and

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:06.950
continue with these list operations.

